.IX Title "File::Flock::Forking 3pm"
.TH File::Flock::Forking 3pm 2026-09-16 "perl v5.40.1" "User Contributed Perl Documentation"
.\" For nroff, turn off justification.  Always turn off hyphenation; it makes
.\" way too many mistakes in technical documents.
.if n .ad l
.nh
.SH NAME
.Vb 1
\& File::Flock::Forking \- adjust File::Flock to handle fork()
.Ve
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IX Header "SYNOPSIS"
.Vb 2
\& use File::Flock::Forking;
\& use File::Flock;
.Ve
.SH DESCRIPTION
.IX Header "DESCRIPTION"
The purpose of File::Flock::Forking is to change the implementation
of File::Flock to handle locking on systems that do not hold
locks across calls to \fBfork()\fR.
.PP
If you are using File::Flock or any module that uses File::Flock
then and your program uses \fBfork()\fR, then you should import
File::Flock::Forking before you import File::Flock or any module that
uses File::Flock.
.PP
On most operating systems, File::Flock::Forking does nothing.  On
Solaris, it changes the behavior of File::Flock to be implemented
by File::Flock::Subprocess.
.PP
You can also force it to use FIle::Flock::Subprocess by with
.PP
.Vb 1
\&        $ENV{FLOCK_FORKING_USE} = \*(Aqsubprocess\*(Aq
.Ve
.PP
Or force it to use File::Flock with
.PP
.Vb 1
\&        $ENV{FLOCK_FORKING_USE} = \*(Aqflock\*(Aq
.Ve
